  "description": "T1: Mature Birch: Approximately 12.00m: This tree overhangs the period conservatory building. Reduce lateral and sub lateral branchesoverhanging the conservatory building by approximately 1.30m to clear. Remove dead wood. Thin density by 20%. General Maintenance.T2: True Acacia: Approximately 9.00m: Also growing adjacent to the conservatory. Reduce lateral and sub lateral growth by approximately 1.00m. Thin crown density 20%. General Maintenance.T3: Mature Cherry: No Further Action.T4: Mature Robinia: Approximately 12.00m: Remove major dead wood only. General maintenance.T5: Norway Maple: Approximately 14.00m: (Ivy clad): Remove ivy from the main crown structure back by approximately 50%, retaining lower ivy mass as screen. Reduce crown by approximately 1.50m-2.00m providing sufficient clearance of the building (at least 1.50m). Remove major dead wood. General maintenance.T6: Conifer: Approximately 7.00m: Growing adjacent to and beneath the canopy of T5: Reduce height by up to approximately 2.00m to increase light levels and separate crowns.General maintenance.T7: Mulberry Tree: Approximately 8.00m: Reduce lateral and sub lateral growth by approximately 1.70m-2.00m.Reduce section of crown overhanging the left hand boundary by up to 1.50m. To balance and help control encroachment.T8: Mature Horse Chestnut: Approximately 21.00m: Left hand corner: Remove major dead wood overhanging the shed and remove dead wood overhanging the boundaries. Raise crown over No.106 by approximately 2.20m-3.00m. Raise low crown skirt over the boundaries by approximately 2.00m-3.00m. General maintenance.T9: Mature Horse chestnut: Approximately 21.00m: The crown spread of this tree is bias over No.104. Carry out minor crown lift of up to 1.50m to match and balance low crown over No.104. General maintenance.",
